Johnson/Desc/ProofFile/Honest/K6/Law/ReaderAtTake.lean: K6's READER AT A TAKE: WHICH ELEMENT OF THE
SENDER's OWN CUT THE ENTRY UNDER A NUMBER OF THE HEAD's MASK ROW IS. Under the library's hypothesis
h, at a place p of the written list of code switches with link 24 and a message n of it (role "own
0", not the last, with a mask row), UNDER hO (the Script's message with that number is the SENDER's
text switchMsgTxt where that answers: ProofFile's form OwnMsgs, at the sender's lot) and public premises:
four fields of the structure of premises K6HeadBelow (Honest/K6/Law/ScriptBelowFree.lean) hold at
the letters o := the switch's own address, d := takeDue p, base := takeBase p n, rowOf :=
rowFromCoin base, own := ownByCut: reader, inside, lent, sender; and, fifth, no default is met below
free.

What is won, and what is not. WON: reader and inside.
lent (stated at chain 0, which is the thread's by
unfolding): a theorem from a public test on the book and the definition ownByCut: it says that the
definition is the cut. sender: met by the choice of the letter rowOf: it buys nothing. hO IS A
PREMISE, PAID BY NOBODY: NO SCRIPT IS LAID, AND NO LOT OF K6's: THE ROW IS THE SENDER's: Q's entries
come in by hO from the sender's text, never from what K6's row spends. hO stands at the thread's own
valid (v = w and Vd v) and at a letter N with the public premise hN, whose right side is the number
of challenges in front of border 341: a form of hO at the rounds in front of an earlier border would
want that this number grows with the border: not called here. THE FIVE CLAUSES ARE FIVE THEOREMS: no
statement has them together; whoever builds a member hands the same hLend to reader and to lent.

The premises. On the written lists (closed facts at one p and n): hp, hlk, hn, hl, hm, hrole. ON THE
BOOK, ONE PUBLIC TEST BY NAME, in the stead of a premise at every run's coins:
hLend: lendsPart (Honest/Small/LendsRow.lean): whether the frame's lendPart
returns at the switch's part: the member for one part of the test lendsRow, since what the sender's
text reads (handed) is lendPart's return. Part 5's test bookCovers (whether the cut is there)
follows from it (covers_of_lends). On public numbers: hN, hf₁, hf₂ (the library's written plan has
H.ns = 3570, Kinds/Law/HeadPlanWritten.lean, not called here: so hf₂ is the narrower fence; with the
field below, which wants 2168, the room for free is 2168 to 3569). On the input's public part: hv.

hLend IS SHOWN AT NO BOOK THAT WE KNOW. Its fourth clause is the lending of the part's carried
runs. AT THE BOOK's MAKER's ELDER TEXT (an earlier text of Honest/BookCarry.lean) IT FAILS AT SUCH
A PART: by its authors' print at the small example the first part of each switch, and no other part
of any kind, listed its carried runs in another order than the book (the same as sets), and the
frame's test "ready" refused the whole honest prover; at the full size, by two houses' programs over
the written list of 189 messages and by no statement in Lean, the orders parted at every switch (the
maker's authors' count), or at 25 of the 26 folds (another house's); the two counts are not squared
here, and whether this switch is the one that did not part is not told. The part here is the first
part of a switch. AT THE MAKER's TURNED TEXT, by its authors' evaluations at the small example: no
part parts from the book in name, count or order, and "ready" returns. AT THE FULL SIZE, OVER THE
TURNED TEXT: NOT SHOWN; that hLend holds at a book that the turned maker returns is stated nowhere
that we know. Where the book does not lend the switch its runs the sender's text answers nowhere (it
begins with what the part is handed), and hO then says nothing. Whether the thread's standing at
border 341 gives the lending by itself is not settled here.

What it is not. That any Script has hO (at a Script laid by lots that holds the sender's lot, the
lots of that number agreeing, ofLots_ownMsgs of Honest/Thread/ScriptLots.lean gives it: not called
